---
// The package: what a team receives when it asks to try this on its own hardware.
//
// Nothing on this page is written here. The bundle is assembled in the engine's repository
// by `./run.sh --release`, and `tools/package.mjs` copies it into the site and writes the
// manifest this page reads, so the numbers, the targets and the prose are the package's own.
//
// The page has one job beyond listing files: saying what the artifact is not. It carries no
// engine source, and a team that expects source should learn that here rather than after
// downloading 4.9 MB.
import Markdown from '../components/Markdown.astro';
import bundle from '../data/package-bundle.json';

const megabytes = (bytes: number) => `${(bytes / 1024 / 1024).toFixed(1)} MB`;
const host = bundle.host ?? bundle.built[bundle.built.length - 1];
const targetOf = (name: string) =>
  name.includes('riscv')
    ? 'RISC-V, no operating system'
    : name.includes('thumb')
      ? 'Cortex-M, no operating system'
      : name.includes('xtensa')
        ? 'ESP32-S3, no operating system'
        : 'This machine, with an operating system';

const values: Record<string, string> = {
  version: bundle.version,
  abi: bundle.abi,
  format: bundle.format,
  entry_points: bundle.entryPoints,
  revision: bundle.revision.replace(/\+$/, ' and a working tree'),
  assembled: bundle.assembled,
  host_target: host ?? 'none',
  target_count: String(bundle.built.length),
  absent_count: String(bundle.absent.length),
};
---

<p class="bench__lede">
  The engine's C surface as a static library and a header, for a firmware team that will link it and not read it. The
  artifact implements the format specification that travels beside it; the engine's own source is not part of the
  package.
</p>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>What this build is</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th></th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>ABI</td><td>{values.abi}, the number a caller's header and the library must agree on</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Format</td><td>{values.format}, the version a volume written by this library carries</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Entry points</td><td>{values.entry_points}, plus the two critical-section hooks the caller defines</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Assembled from</td><td>revision {values.revision}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Targets</td><td>{values.target_count} built, {values.absent_count} not built on the host that assembled it</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>The artifacts</h2>
  {
    !bundle.served && (
      <p>
        The package is assembled and its address is not published yet. What follows is what is in it, measured from
        the bundle itself; the download address arrives when the package is served from one.
      </p>
    )
  }
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Target</th><th>What it is for</th>{bundle.served && <th></th>}<th>Size</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        bundle.targets.map((tarball: { target: string; href: string | null; bytes: number }) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{tarball.target}</code></td>
            <td>{targetOf(tarball.target)}</td>
            {bundle.served && <td>{tarball.href ? <a href={tarball.href}>download</a> : '—'}</td>}
            <td>{megabytes(tarball.bytes)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {
    bundle.absent.length > 0 && (
      <p>
        Not built on the host that assembled this release, and not claimed here:{' '}
        {bundle.absent.map((target: string, index: number) => (
          <>
            {index > 0 && ', '}
            <code>{target}</code>
          </>
        ))}
        . Its toolchain is not part of the Rust distribution, so it is packaged where that toolchain
        is installed.
      </p>
    )
  }
</section>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>Five minutes, on a machine that has an operating system</h2>
  <p>
    The archive for a hosted target holds a program that links and runs. Unpack it and build it with the compiler you
    already have; nothing else is needed and nothing is installed.
  </p>
  <pre><code>tar -xzf {host}.tar.gz
cd {host}
cc -I include example.c lib/libktema_capi.a -o example && ./example
echo $?        # 0 is four records written and walked</code></pre>
  <p>
    On a device, the same directory gives you the header and the library for your target. The medium in
    <code>example.c</code> is a table in RAM; replace it with your own read, write, list and delete, supply
    <code>ktma_enter_critical</code> and <code>ktma_exit_critical</code>, and link the object. The header states what the
    engine expects of that medium, and <code>LINKING</code> in each target directory states what the link owes.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>The documents in the package</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th>{bundle.served && <th></th>}<th></th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        bundle.documents.map((document: { label: string; href: string | null; bytes: number }) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{document.label}</td>
            {bundle.served && <td>{document.href ? <a href={document.href}>open</a> : '—'}</td>}
            <td>{document.bytes < 4096 ? `${document.bytes} B` : megabytes(document.bytes)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <code>SHA256SUMS</code> covers every file in the package. The digests are reproducible: assembling the same
    revision twice produces the same bytes, and the date the manifest carries is the commit's rather than the clock's.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>What the ABI number promises</h2>
  <Markdown source={bundle.changelog} />
</section>

<section class="bench__fit">
  <h2>What is not here</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The engine's source. The package is a compiled artifact, a header, and the specification it implements.</li>
    <li>A driver. The medium is the caller's, because the driver a device already has is the one that should be used.</li>
    <li>A claim about another store. The package carries no benchmark and no comparison.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This page is generated from the package: <code>./run.sh --release</code> in the engine's repository assembles it,
    and the site copies what it assembled.
  </p>
</section>
